+# On-call runbook
+
+For whoever holds the tally pager. All commands use `tallyctl`. The old name `tally-cli` is an alias that goes away
+after one more release.
+
+Before your first shift: pages come through Beacon (blue owl icon). Install the app and log in with SSO.
+
+## Escalation path
+
+Every page goes to the primary first. If nobody acknowledges within 15 minutes, it moves to the next person.
+Acknowledging is a tap on the phone, so do that first and then get to a laptop.
+
+| Step | Who | Paged when |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Primary on-call | Immediately |
+| 2 | Secondary on-call | The primary has not acknowledged after 15 minutes |
+| 3 | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | The secondary has not acknowledged after another 15 minutes |
+
+The manager is a real step, not a courtesy.
+
+**Security-related issues:** do not go through the secondary. Page the security lead directly.
+
+## When to page at night
+
+Between 22:00 and 07:00 the pager is used only for severity 1.
+
+- **Severity 1:** customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Pages at any hour.
+- **Severity 2:** for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch. Does not page at night. It waits for business hours
+  and the primary picks it up in the morning.
+
+This rule is for the pager. It is not a licence to ignore the dashboards.
+
+## While you work
+
+Write down the commands you run while you run them. Reconstructing them afterwards is the slowest part of a postmortem.
+
+```
+tallyctl status                              # state of every service; --watch refreshes every two seconds
+tallyctl logs tail --follow --service NAME   # stream one service's logs
+tallyctl certs list                          # certificates with their expiry dates
+```
+
+## Restarting services
+
+`tallyctl restart <service>` restarts one service. It gives up after 120 seconds by default; change that with
+`--timeout SECONDS`.
+
+| Service | Command | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| API | `tallyctl restart api` | Also required after renewing the API certificate |
+| Ingest | `tallyctl restart ingest` | Plain restart |
+| Ingest (stuck queue) | `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` | Waits for in-flight work, parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table, then restarts. `--drain` works for ingest only |
+| Worker | `tallyctl restart worker` | |
+| Scheduler | `tallyctl restart scheduler` | |
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck (poisoned batch)
+
+**Symptoms:** the queue depth alert fires. The ingest workers are running and look healthy, but the queue has stopped
+draining and its oldest message has been sitting there for hours. Dashboards go stale.
+
+**Cause:** one malformed batch wedges the consumer, which keeps retrying it and never moves on. A plain restart does
+not help, because the consumer picks up the same batch again on startup.
+
+**Fix:**
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+Do the drain restart first and look for the cause afterwards. In August the first half hour went on checking the
+network, and the network was fine. The parked batch stays in the dead-letter table and can be inspected the next day.
+
+### Database host disk full
+
+**Symptoms:** the disk on the db host fills up. At 97 percent the database refuses writes. This often follows an ingest
+backlog, because the retries write a lot of log and the replay bloats the tables.
+
+**Fix, in this order:**
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+- Vacuum first, because it needs a little scratch space and the rotation does not.
+- Always use `--online` in production, because it does not block writers. A plain `tallyctl db vacuum` locks the
+  tables, and in August it made things worse while ingest was writing. `--full` needs the service stopped.
+- Rotation compresses old log files and removes the ones past retention. It keeps the last 14 by default
+  (`--keep N`).
+- Do not wait for 97 percent. In September the same two commands took the disk from 85 to 55 percent with no
+  incident.
+- Clearing the cache was tried in August, did nothing useful, and is not part of the fix.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Symptoms:** the public API answers with certificate errors. Browsers refuse to connect, and so does every client
+that checks certificates. The load balancer, DNS and the latest deployment all look fine.
+
+**Fix:**
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list           # read the expiry date first
+tallyctl certs renew api      # --dry-run shows what would happen
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+Renewing takes two steps, and the restart is not optional. Until you restart, the service keeps serving the old
+certificate, which makes it look as though the renewal failed. In September the checks went green within a minute of
+the restart. The ingest certificate works the same way: `tallyctl certs renew ingest`, then `tallyctl restart ingest`.
+
+List the certificates early. In September nobody looked at them until 26 minutes into the outage. The renewal job now
+raises an alert when it fails.
+
+### Leftovers from the 2026-09 release
+
+- **Connection refused on port 8080:** the service moved to 8731 and 8080 is closed. Fix the script, health check or
+  bookmark. `tallyctl` uses 8731 unless `--host` says otherwise.
+- **"Unknown subcommand" from the old cache purge command:** that command was removed. Use `tallyctl cache clear`
+  (all regions) or `tallyctl cache clear --region NAME`.
